Final answer: ### 1. Task outcome (short version):
Screened 100 studies: 24 INCLUDED, 76 EXCLUDED

### 2. Task outcome (extremely detailed version):

TITLE/ABSTRACT SCREENING RESULTS FOR CHUNK (Studies 1-100)

Review: Early and late adverse renal effects after potentially nephrotoxic treatment for childhood cancer

SUMMARY:
- Total studies screened: 100
- Studies INCLUDED: 24
- Studies EXCLUDED: 76

INCLUDED STUDIES (24):

Study 1: Impact of nephrectomy on long‐term renal function in non‐syndromic children treated for unifocal Wilms tumor
  Decision: INCLUDE
  Confidence: high
  Rationale: Retrospective cohort study of 55 children treated with nephrectomy for Wilms tumor. Reports on long-term 
renal function (GFR) with median follow-up of 7-11 years. Meets all criteria: childhood cancer survivors, nephrectomy 
treatment, >20 participants, renal function assessed >1 year post-treatment.

Study 3: Renal function after autologous bone marrow transplantation
  Decision: INCLUDE
  Confidence: medium
  Rationale: Study of 72 patients after ABMT (41 with TBI). Reports on renal function (GFR, creatinine, urea). While 
primary assessment was at 6 months, abstract mentions 'some patients have later improved considerably' suggesting longer
follow-up. TBI involves kidney region. Conservative inclusion due to potential long-term data.

Study 9: Long‐term renal toxicity in children following fractionated total‐body irradiation (TBI) before allogeneic stem
cell transplantation (SCT)
  Decision: INCLUDE
  Confidence: high
  Rationale: Retrospective study of 92 children (≤16 years) who underwent TBI before allogeneic SCT. 43 patients had ≥12
months follow-up (median 51 months). Reports on renal dysfunction defined by serum creatinine. TBI involves kidney 
region. Meets all criteria: childhood cancer survivors, radiation involving kidney region, >20 participants, renal 
function assessed >1 year post-treatment.

Study 11: High‐dose chemotherapy with autologous stem cell rescue in children with nephroblastoma
  Decision: INCLUDE
  Confidence: medium
  Rationale: Study of 23 children with Wilms tumor receiving high-dose chemotherapy including carboplatin. Median 
observation 41 months. Reports on renal toxicity (tubular dysfunction). While renal function monitoring was during 
treatment, the study follows patients long-term. Conservative inclusion as it reports on renal outcomes in childhood 
cancer survivors treated with carboplatin.

Study 12: Health status at long‐term follow‐up in patients treated for extremity localized Ewing sarcoma or 
osteosarcoma: a Scandinavian Sarcoma Group study
  Decision: INCLUDE
  Confidence: medium
  Rationale: Study of 133 long-term survivors (>5 years) of extremity bone sarcoma. Median follow-up 12 years. Reports 
that 13% had reduced renal function. Osteosarcoma and Ewing sarcoma treatments typically include cisplatin and 
ifosfamide. Meets criteria: childhood cancer survivors, >20 participants, renal function reported >1 year 
post-treatment.

Study 15: Renal failure in Wilms' tumor patients: a report from the National Wilms' Tumor Study Group
  Decision: INCLUDE
  Confidence: high
  Rationale: Report from NWTS database identifying 55 children who developed chronic renal failure during/after Wilms 
tumor treatment. Median interval from diagnosis to RF onset was 21 months (>1 year). Etiologies include bilateral 
nephrectomy, radiation nephritis. Meets criteria: childhood cancer survivors, nephrectomy/radiation treatments, >20 
participants, renal outcomes reported.

Study 27: Estimation of ifosfamide/cisplatinum‐induced renal toxicity by urinary protein analysis
  Decision: INCLUDE
  Confidence: medium
  Rationale: Study of 64 patients receiving ifosfamide with or without cisplatin. Reports on glomerular and tubular 
function after completion of chemotherapy. While timing of assessment is not explicitly stated as >1 year, the study 
evaluates renal toxicity after treatment completion. Conservative inclusion as it reports on renal function after 
nephrotoxic treatment.

Study 35: Long‐term follow‐up of cancer in neonates and infants: a national survey of 142 patients
  Decision: INCLUDE
  Confidence: medium
  Rationale: National survey of 142 patients (79 survivors) treated for solid malignant tumors before age 1 year. Mean 
follow-up 20 years. Reports renal damage as one of late side effects. Includes patients treated with nephrectomy, 
chemotherapy, and radiotherapy. Meets criteria: childhood cancer survivors, >20 participants, long-term follow-up, renal
outcomes reported.

Study 36: Renal toxicity of ifosfamide in pilot regimens of the intergroup rhabdomyosarcoma study for patients with 
gross residual tumor
  Decision: INCLUDE
  Confidence: medium
  Rationale: Study evaluating nephrotoxicity in patients under 21 years treated with ifosfamide-containing chemotherapy 
for rhabdomyosarcoma. Abstract is truncated but indicates focus on renal toxicity after ifosfamide treatment. 
Conservative inclusion as it appears to report on renal outcomes in childhood cancer survivors treated with ifosfamide.

Study 39: Long‐term renal function in unilateral non‐syndromic renal tumor survivors treated according to International 
Society of Pediatric Oncology Protocols
  Decision: INCLUDE
  Confidence: high
  Rationale: Study of 35 adult survivors (mean age 25 years, mean follow-up 20 years) of unilateral non-syndromic renal 
tumors treated according to SIOP protocols. Reports on eGFR categories, CKD, and hypertension. Meets criteria: childhood
cancer survivors (treated as children), nephrectomy treatment, >20 participants, renal function assessed >1 year 
post-treatment.

Study 44: Reassessing dose constraints of organs at risk in children with abdominal neuroblastoma treated with 
definitive radiation therapy: a correlation with late toxicity
  Decision: INCLUDE
  Confidence: high
  Rationale: Study of 30 children with abdominal neuroblastoma receiving definitive RT. Median follow-up 53 months. 
Reports on late kidney toxicity (2 patients with ipsilateral kidney hypoplasia). Radiation involves kidney region. Meets
criteria: childhood cancer survivors, radiation involving kidney region, >20 participants, renal outcomes reported >1 
year post-treatment.

Study 45: Development of hypertension is less frequent after bilateral nephron sparing surgery for bilateral Wilms tumor
in a long‐term survey
  Decision: INCLUDE
  Confidence: high
  Rationale: Study of 22 patients with bilateral Wilms tumor comparing bilateral partial nephrectomy vs unilateral 
partial plus contralateral total nephrectomy. Reports on renal hypertension outcomes. Meets criteria: childhood cancer 
survivors, nephrectomy treatment, >20 participants, renal outcomes (hypertension) reported.

Study 48: Long‐term followup of renal functional reserve capacity after unilateral nephrectomy in childhood
  Decision: INCLUDE
  Confidence: high
  Rationale: Study of 37 subjects who underwent unilateral nephrectomy during childhood (age <16 years). Long-term 
